// What a type parameter's constraint names: what a type bound to the
// parameter must be. Each is a constraint only, and no type a value may have;
// an alias names one under another name, `struct Formattable =
// methodsOf[struct { const func format() string }]`.

// Says nothing about the type: `func first[T any](xs list[T]) option[T]`.
#[_builtin(any)]
struct any {}

// A struct, whatever its fields: what a record of named values is passed as,
// `func get[Q anyStruct](url string, query Q)`. A component is no struct.
#[_builtin(anyStruct)]
struct anyStruct {}

// A type with fields, a struct or a component, whatever they are.
#[_builtin(anyFields)]
struct anyFields {}

// A type `<` orders: a number, a single-base unit or a string. A generic body
// may compare two values of the type, `func least[T ordered](a T, b T) => a <
// b ? a : b`, and a list of one sorts, `xs.sort()`.
#[_builtin(ordered)]
struct ordered {}

// A type with each of S's fields, at an equal type, and maybe others: a
// struct or a component. S is an anonymous struct of fields, and they are
// what a generic body may read of a value of the type: `[T fieldsOf[struct {
// name string }]]` reads a T's name.
#[_builtin(fieldsOf)]
struct fieldsOf[S any] {}

// A type with a method fitting each function S declares, whatever else it
// is: an int as readily as a struct. S is an anonymous struct of functions,
// conventionally without bodies. A method fits one when it takes each of its
// parameters by name at an equal type, defaults the rest, gives what it gives
// and is const where it is; a parameter's default there is a constant the
// method defaults to as well.
#[_builtin(methodsOf)]
struct methodsOf[S any] {}
